ud2.6:1.5 | S: “Go, brahmin, bring oil for my delivery.” | T: “Go, brahman, get some oil for my delivery.”
ud2.6:2.1 | S: The wanderer said, | T: When this was said, the wanderer said to her,
ud2.6:2.2 | S: “But where, my dear, can I get oil?” | T: “But where can I get any oil?”
ud2.6:2.3 | S: For a second time, she said, | T: A second time, she said to him,
ud2.6:2.4 | S: “Go, brahmin, bring oil for my delivery.” | T: “Go, brahman, get some oil for my delivery.”
ud2.6:2.5 | S: For a second time, the wanderer said, | T: A second time, he said to her,
ud2.6:2.6 | S: “But where, my dear, can I get oil?” | T: “But where can I get any oil?”
ud2.6:2.7 | S: For a third time, she said, | T: A third time, she said to him,
ud2.6:2.8 | S: “Go, brahmin, bring oil for my delivery.” | T: “Go, brahman get some oil for my delivery.”
ud2.6:3.1 | S: Now at that time ghee and oil were being given away to any ascetic or brahmin at the storehouse of King Pasenadi of Kosala. But it was only to drink there, not to take away. | T: Now on that occasion at the storehouse of King Pasenadi Kosala contemplatives & brahmans were being given as much oil or ghee as they needed to drink, but not to take away.
ud2.6:4.1 | S: Knowing this, the wanderer thought, | T: So the thought occurred to the wanderer,
ud2.6:4.2 | P: “rañño kho pana pasenadissa kosalassa koṭṭhāgāre samaṇassa vā brāhmaṇassa vā sappissa vā telassa vā yāvadatthaṁ pātuṁ dīyati, no nīharituṁ. | T: “At present at the storehouse of King Pasenadi Kosala contemplatives & brahmans are being given as much oil or ghee as they need to drink, but not to take away.
ud2.6:4.3 | S: “Why don’t I go to the king’s storehouse, drink as much oil as I can, then come home and throw it up so it can be used for the delivery?” | T: Suppose, having gone there, I were to drink as much oil as I need and, on returning home, vomiting it up, were to give it to use at this delivery?”
ud2.6:5.1 | S: Then he did just that. But when he got home he was unable to either bring it up or pass it out. | T: So, having gone to the storehouse of King Pasenadi Kosala, he drank as much oil as he needed but, on returning home, was unable to bring it up or pass it down.
ud2.6:5.2 | S: He rolled to and fro, suffering painful, sharp, severe, acute feelings. | T: So he rolled back & forth, suffering from fierce pains, sharp & severe.
ud2.6:6.1 | S: Then the Buddha robed up in the morning and, taking his bowl and robe, entered Sāvatthī for alms. | T: Then early in the morning the Blessed One adjusted his under robe and–carrying his bowl & robes–went into Sāvatthī for alms.
ud2.6:6.2 | S: He saw the wanderer in agony. | T: He saw the wanderer rolling back & forth, suffering from fierce pains, sharp & severe.
ud2.6:7.1 | S: Then, understanding this matter, on that occasion the Buddha expressed this heartfelt sentiment: | T: Then, on realizing the significance of that, the Blessed One on that occasion exclaimed:
