## ud3.2
ud3.2:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: I have heard that
ud3.2:1.2 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery. | T: on one occasion the Blessed One was staying near Sāvatthī at Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery.
ud3.2:1.3 | S: Now at that time Venerable Nanda, the Buddha’s brother and maternal cousin, informed several mendicants: | T: And on that occasion Ven. Nanda–the Blessed One’s brother, son of his maternal aunt–announced to a large number of monks:
ud3.2:1.4 | S: “I lead the spiritual life dissatisfied. | T: “I don’t enjoy leading the holy life, my friends.
ud3.2:1.5 | S: I am unable to keep up the spiritual life. I shall resign the training and return to a lesser life.” | T: I can’t keep up the holy life. Giving up the training, I will return to the common life.”
ud3.2:2.1 | S: Then a mendicant went up to the Buddha, bowed, sat down to one side, and told him what was happening. | T: Then a certain monk went to the Blessed One and, on arrival, having bowed down to him, sat to one side. As he was sitting there, he told the Blessed One:
ud3.2:2.2 | P: “āyasmā, bhante, nando bhagavato bhātā mātucchāputto sambahulānaṁ bhikkhūnaṁ evamāroceti: | T: “Lord, Ven. Nanda–the Blessed One’s brother, son of his maternal aunt–has announced to a large number of monks:
ud3.2:2.3 | P: ‘anabhirato ahaṁ, āvuso, brahmacariyaṁ carāmi, na sakkomi brahmacariyaṁ sandhāretuṁ, sikkhaṁ paccakkhāya hīnāyāvattissāmī’”ti. | T: ‘I don’t enjoy leading the holy life, my friends. I can’t keep up the holy life. Giving up the training, I will return to the common life.’”
ud3.2:3.1 | S: So the Buddha addressed one of the monks, | T: Then the Blessed One told a certain monk,
ud3.2:3.2 | S: “Please, monk, in my name tell the mendicant Nanda that | T: “Come, monk. In my name, call Nanda, saying,
ud3.2:3.3 | S: the Teacher summons him.” | T: ‘The Teacher calls you, friend Nanda.’”
ud3.2:3.4 | S: “Yes, sir,” that monk replied. He went to Nanda and said to him, | T: Responding, “As you say, lord,” to the Blessed One, the monk went to Ven. Nanda, on arrival he said,
ud3.2:3.5 | S: “Reverend Nanda, the Teacher summons you.” | T: “The Teacher calls you, friend Nanda.”
ud3.2:4.1 | S: “Yes, reverend,” Nanda replied. He went to the Buddha, bowed, and sat down to one side. The Buddha said to him: | T: Responding, “As you say, my friend,” to the monk, Ven. Nanda went to the Blessed One and, on arrival, having bowed down to him, sat to one side. As he was sitting there, the Blessed One said to him,
ud3.2:5.1 | S: “Is it really true, Nanda, that you informed several mendicants that | T: “Is it true, Nanda, that you have announced to a large number of monks:
ud3.2:5.2 | S: you are unable to keep up the spiritual life; that you shall resign the training and return to a lesser life?” | T: ‘I don’t enjoy leading the holy life, my friends. I can’t keep up the holy life. Giving up the training, I will return to the common life’?”
ud3.2:5.3 | S: “Yes, sir,” he replied. | T: “Yes, lord.”
ud3.2:6.1 | S: “But why are you so dissatisfied with the spiritual life?” | T: “But why, Nanda, don’t you enjoy leading the holy life? Why can’t you keep up the holy life? Why, giving up the training, will you return to the common life?”
ud3.2:6.2 | S: “As I left my house, sir, the finest lady of the Sakyan land, her hair half-combed, glanced at me and said, | T: “Lord, as I was leaving home, a Sakyan girl–the envy of the countryside–glanced up at me, with her hair half-combed, and said,
ud3.2:6.3 | S: ‘Hurry back, master.’ | T: ‘Hurry back, master.’
ud3.2:6.4 | S: Recalling that, I am dissatisfied and shall resign the training.” | T: Recollecting that, I don’t enjoy leading the holy life. I can’t keep up the holy life. Giving up the training, I will return to the common life.”
ud3.2:7.1 | S: Then the Buddha took Nanda by the arm and, as easily as a strong person would extend or contract their arm, vanished from Jeta’s Grove and reappeared among the gods of the thirty-three. | T: Then, taking Ven. Nanda by the arm–as a strong man might flex his extended arm or extend his flexed arm–the Blessed One disappeared from Jeta’s Grove and reappeared among the Devas of the Heaven of the Thirty-three [Tāvatiṁsa].
ud3.2:8.1 | S: Now at that time five hundred dove-footed nymphs had come to attend to Sakka, the lord of gods. | T: Now on that occasion about 500 dove-footed nymphs had come to wait upon Sakka, the ruler of the devas.
ud3.2:8.2 | S: Then the Buddha said to Nanda, | T: The Blessed One said to Ven. Nanda,
ud3.2:8.3 | S: “Nanda, do you see these five hundred dove-footed nymphs?” | T: “Nanda, do you see these 500 dove-footed nymphs?”
ud3.2:8.4 | S: “Yes, sir,” he replied. | T: “Yes, lord.”
ud3.2:9.1 | S: “What do you think, Nanda? Who is more attractive, good-looking, and lovely—the finest lady of the Sakyan land, or these five hundred dove-footed nymphs?” | T: “What do you think, Nanda? Which is lovelier, better looking, more charming: the Sakyan girl, the envy of the countryside, or these 500 dove-footed nymphs?”
ud3.2:9.2 | S: “Compared to these five hundred dove-footed nymphs, the finest lady of the Sakyan land is like a deformed monkey with its ears and nose cut off. | T: “Lord, compared to these 500 dove-footed nymphs, the Sakyan girl, the envy of the countryside, is like a cauterized monkey with its ears & nose cut off.
ud3.2:9.3 | S: She doesn’t count, there’s no comparison, she’s not worth a fraction. | T: She doesn’t count. She’s not even a small fraction. There’s no comparison.
ud3.2:9.4 | S: These five hundred dove-footed nymphs are far more attractive, good-looking, and lovely.” | T: The 500 dove-footed nymphs are lovelier, better looking, more charming.”
ud3.2:10.1 | S: “Rejoice, Nanda, rejoice! | T: “Then take joy, Nanda. Take joy!
ud3.2:10.2 | S: I guarantee you five hundred dove-footed nymphs.” | T: I am your guarantor for getting 500 dove-footed nymphs.”
ud3.2:10.3 | S: “If, sir, you guarantee me five hundred dove-footed nymphs, I shall happily lead the spiritual life under the Buddha.” | T: “If the Blessed One is my guarantor for getting 500 dove-footed nymphs, I will enjoy leading the holy life under the Blessed One.”
ud3.2:11.1 | S: Then the Buddha took Nanda by the arm and, as easily as a strong person would extend or contract their arm, vanished from the gods of the thirty-three and reappeared at Jeta’s Grove. | T: Then, taking Ven. Nanda by the arm–as a strong man might flex his extended arm or extend his flexed arm–the Blessed One disappeared from among the Devas of the Heaven of the Thirty-three and reappeared at Jeta’s Grove.
ud3.2:12.1 | S: The mendicants heard, | T: The monks heard,
ud3.2:12.2 | S: “It seems Venerable Nanda—who is both the Buddha’s half-brother and maternal cousin—leads the spiritual life for the sake of nymphs. | T: “They say that Ven. Nanda–the Blessed One’s brother, son of his maternal aunt–is leading the holy life for the sake of nymphs.
ud3.2:12.3 | S: And it seems that the Buddha guaranteed him five hundred dove-footed nymphs.” | T: They say that the Blessed One is his guarantor for getting 500 dove-footed nymphs.”
ud3.2:13.1 | S: Monks who were his friends accused him of being a hireling and a lackey, | T: Then the monks who were companions of Ven. Nanda went around addressing him as they would a hired hand & a person who had been bought:
ud3.2:13.2 | S: “It seems Nanda is a hireling, it seems he is a lackey: he leads the spiritual life for the sake of nymphs. | T: “Venerable Nanda, they say, has been hired. Venerable Nanda, they say, has been bought. He’s leading the holy life for the sake of nymphs.
ud3.2:13.3 | S: And it seems that the Buddha guaranteed him five hundred dove-footed nymphs.” | T: The Blessed One is his guarantor for getting 500 dove-footed nymphs.”
ud3.2:14.1 | S: Then Nanda—embarrassed, ashamed, and disgusted at being called a hireling and a lackey—living alone, withdrawn, diligent, keen, and resolute, soon realized the supreme culmination of the spiritual path in this very life. He lived having achieved with his own insight the goal for which gentlemen rightly go forth from the lay life to homelessness. | T: Then Ven. Nanda–humiliated, ashamed, & disgusted that the monks who were his companions were addressing him as they would a hired hand & a person who had been bought–went to dwell alone, secluded, heedful, ardent, & resolute. He in no long time entered & remained in the supreme goal of the holy life for which clansmen rightly go forth from home into homelessness, knowing & realizing it for himself right in the here-&-now.
ud3.2:14.2 | S: He understood: “Rebirth is ended; the spiritual journey has been completed; what had to be done has been done; there is nothing further for this place.” | T: He knew, “Birth is ended, the holy life fulfilled, the task done. There is nothing further for the sake of this world.”
ud3.2:14.3 | S: Venerable Nanda became one of the perfected. | T: And thus Ven. Nanda became another one of the arahants.
ud3.2:15.1 | S: Then, late at night, a glorious deity, lighting up the entire Jeta’s Grove, went up to the Buddha, bowed, stood to one side, and said to him: | T: Then a certain devatā, in the far extreme of the night, her extreme radiance lighting up the entirety of Jeta’s Grove, approached the Blessed One. On arrival, having bowed down to him, she stood to one side. As she was standing there, she said to the Blessed One,
ud3.2:15.2 | S: “Sir, Venerable Nanda—who is both the Buddha’s half-brother and maternal cousin—has realized the undefiled freedom of heart and freedom by wisdom. He lives having realized it with his own insight in this very life due to the ending of defilements.” | T: “Lord, Ven. Nanda–the Blessed One’s brother, son of his maternal aunt–through the ending of the effluents, has entered & remains in the effluent-free awareness-release & discernment-release, directly knowing & realizing them for himself right in the here-&-now.”
ud3.2:15.3 | S: And the knowledge also came to the Buddha: | T: And within the Blessed One, the knowledge arose:
ud3.2:15.4 | S: “Nanda has realized the undefiled freedom of heart and freedom by wisdom. He lives having realized it with his own insight in this very life due to the ending of defilements.” | T: “Nanda, through the ending of the effluents, has entered & remains in the effluent-free awareness-release & discernment-release, directly knowing & realizing them for himself right in the here-&-now.”
ud3.2:16.1 | S: Then, when the night had passed, Nanda went to the Buddha, bowed, sat down to one side, and said to him, | T: Then, when the night had passed, Ven. Nanda went to the Blessed One and, on arrival, having bowed down to him, sat to one side. As he was sitting there, he said to the Blessed One,
ud3.2:16.2 | S: “Worthy sir, you guaranteed me five hundred dove-footed nymphs. I release you from that promise.” | T: “Lord, about the Blessed One’s being my guarantor for getting 500 dove-footed nymphs: I hereby release the Blessed One from that promise.”
ud3.2:16.3 | S: “Nanda, I encompassed your mind and knew that | T: “Nanda, having comprehended your awareness with my own awareness, I realized that
ud3.2:16.4 | S: you had realized the undefiled freedom of heart and freedom by wisdom. | T: ‘Nanda, through the ending of the effluents, has entered & remains in the effluent-free awareness-release & discernment-release, directly knowing & realizing them for himself right in the here-&-now.’
ud3.2:16.5 | S: And deities also told me about this. | T: And a devatā informed me that
ud3.2:16.6 | P: ‘āyasmā, bhante, nando bhagavato bhātā mātucchāputto āsavānaṁ khayā anāsavaṁ cetovimuttiṁ paññāvimuttiṁ diṭṭheva dhamme sayaṁ abhiññā sacchikatvā upasampajja viharatī’ti. | T: ‘Ven. Nanda, through the ending of the effluents, has entered & remains in the effluent-free awareness-release & discernment-release, directly knowing & realizing them for himself right in the here-&-now.’
ud3.2:16.7 | S: As soon as your mind was freed from defilements by not grasping, I was released from that promise.” | T: When your mind, through lack of clinging, was released from the effluents, I was thereby released from that promise.”
ud3.2:17.1 | S: Then, understanding this matter, on that occasion the Buddha expressed this heartfelt sentiment: | T: Then, on realizing the significance of that, the Blessed One on that occasion exclaimed:
ud3.2:18.1 | S: “The mendicant who has crossed over the bog, | T: In whom the mire of sensuality is crossed over,
ud3.2:18.2 | S: who has crushed the thorn of sensuality, | T: the thorn of sensuality crushed,

