 succeeded in 0ms:
ud3.2:18.3 | S: who has reached the end of delusion, | T: the ending of delusion reached:
ud3.2:18.4 | S: trembles not at pleasure and pain.” | T: He doesn’t quiver from pleasures & pains : a monk.
ud3.2:18.5 | P: Dutiyaṁ. | T: (none)

## ud3.3
ud3.3:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: I have heard that
ud3.3:1.2 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery. | T: on one occasion the Blessed One was staying near Sāvatthī at Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery.
ud3.3:1.3 | S: Now at that time five hundred mendicants headed by Yasoja arrived at Sāvatthī to see the Buddha. | T: And on that occasion approximately 500 monks, headed by Ven. Yasoja, had arrived in Sāvatthī to see the Blessed One.
ud3.3:1.4 | S: At that, those visiting mendicants, while exchanging pleasantries with the resident mendicants, preparing their lodgings, and putting away their bowls and robes, made a colossal racket. | T: As these visiting monks were exchanging greetings with the resident monks, setting their lodgings in order, and putting away their robes & bowls, they made a loud racket, a great racket.
ud3.3:2.1 | S: Then the Buddha said to Venerable Ānanda, | T: Then the Blessed One said to Ven. Ānanda,
ud3.3:2.2 | S: “Ānanda, who’s making that colossal racket? You’d think it was fishermen hauling in a catch!” | T: “Ānanda, what is that loud racket, that great racket like fishermen with a catch of fish?”
ud3.3:2.3 | S: “Sir, those five hundred mendicants headed by Yasoja have arrived at Sāvatthī to see the Buddha. | T: “Lord, those are approximately 500 monks, headed by Ven. Yasoja, who have arrived in Sāvatthī to see the Blessed One.
ud3.3:2.4 | S: It’s those visiting mendicants who, while exchanging pleasantries with the resident mendicants, preparing their lodgings, and putting away their bowls and robes, made a colossal racket.” | T: As these visiting monks are exchanging greetings with the resident monks, setting their lodgings in order, and putting away their robes & bowls, they are making a loud racket, a great racket.”
ud3.3:2.5 | S: “Well then, Ānanda, in my name tell those mendicants that | T: “In that case, Ānanda, tell those monks in my name,
ud3.3:2.6 | S: the Teacher summons them.” | T: ‘The Teacher calls you, friends.’”
ud3.3:3.1 | S: “Yes, sir,” Ānanda replied. He went to those mendicants and said, | T: Responding, “As you say, lord,” to the Blessed One, Ven. Ānanda went to the monks and said,
ud3.3:3.2 | S: “Venerables, the Teacher summons you.” | T: “The Teacher calls you, friends.”
ud3.3:3.3 | S: “Yes, reverend,” replied those mendicants. Then they rose from their seats and went to the Buddha, bowed, and sat down to one side. The Buddha said to them, | T: Responding, “As you say, friend,” to Ven. Ānanda, the monks went to the Blessed One and, on arrival, having bowed down to him, sat to one side. As they were sitting there, the Blessed One said to them,
ud3.3:4.1 | S: “Mendicants, what’s with that colossal racket? You’d think it was fishermen hauling in a catch!” | T: “Monks, why were you making that loud racket, that great racket, like fishermen with a catch of fish?”
ud3.3:4.2 | S: When he said this, Venerable Yasoja said to the Buddha, | T: When this was said, Ven. Yasoja said to the Blessed One,
ud3.3:4.3 | S: “Sir, these five hundred mendicants have arrived at Sāvatthī to see the Buddha. | T: “Lord, these 500 monks have arrived in Sāvatthī to see the Blessed One.
ud3.3:4.4 | S: It’s these visiting mendicants who, while exchanging pleasantries with the resident mendicants, preparing their lodgings, and putting away their bowls and robes, made a colossal racket.” | T: As they were exchanging greetings with the resident monks, setting their lodgings in order, and putting away their robes & bowls, they made a loud racket, a great racket.”
ud3.3:4.5 | S: “Go away, mendicants, I dismiss you. You are not to stay in my presence.” | T: “Go away, monks. I dismiss you. You are not to stay in my vicinity.”
ud3.3:5.1 | S: “Yes, sir,” replied those mendicants. They got up from their seats, bowed and respectfully circled the Buddha, keeping him on their right. They set their lodgings in order and left, taking their bowls and robes. | T: Responding, “As you say, lord,” to the Blessed One, the monks got up from their seats, bowed down to the Blessed One, and left, circling him to the right. Setting their lodgings in order and taking their robes & bowls, they went wandering among the Vajjians.
ud3.3:5.2 | S: Traveling stage by stage in the land of the Vajjis, they arrived at the Vaggumudā River. They built leaf huts near the riverbank and there they entered the rainy season. | T: After wandering by stages among the Vajjians, they came to the River Vaggamudā. There on the bank of the River Vaggamudā they made leaf-huts and entered the Rains Retreat.
ud3.3:6.1 | S: Then Venerable Yasoja, having entered the rainy season, addressed the mendicants: | T: Then Ven. Yasoja addressed the monks as they entered the Rains Retreat:
ud3.3:6.2 | S: “Out of sympathy, reverends, the Buddha dismissed us, wanting what’s best for us. | T: “Friends, the Blessed One dismissed us, wishing for our benefit, seeking our wellbeing, being sympathetic, and acting out of sympathy.
ud3.3:6.3 | S: Come, let us live in such a way that the Buddha would be pleased with us.” | T: Let’s live in such a way that the Blessed One will be gratified by our way of living.”
ud3.3:6.4 | S: “Yes, reverend,” they replied. | T: “As you say, friend,” the monks responded to Ven. Yasoja.
ud3.3:6.5 | S: Then those mendicants, living alone, withdrawn, diligent, keen, and resolute, all realized the three knowledges in that same rainy season. | T: And so, living secluded, ardent, & resolute, every one of them realized the Three Knowledges [remembrance of past lives, knowledge of the arising & passing away of living beings, and knowledge of the ending of mental effluents] in the course of that very Rains Retreat.
ud3.3:7.1 | S: When the Buddha had stayed in Sāvatthī as long as he pleased, he set out for Vesālī. | T: Then the Blessed One, having stayed as long as he liked in Sāvatthī, went wandering in the direction of Vesālī.
ud3.3:7.2 | S: Traveling stage by stage, he arrived at Vesālī, | T: After wandering by stages, he arrived in Vesālī
ud3.3:7.3 | S: where he stayed in the hall with the peaked roof. | T: and stayed there in the Gabled Pavilion in the Great Forest.
ud3.3:8.1 | S: Then, having applied his mind to comprehending the minds of the mendicants staying on the bank of the Vaggumudā River, the Buddha said to Venerable Ānanda, | T: Then, encompassing with his awareness the awareness of the monks staying on the bank of the River Vaggamudā, he said to Ven. Ānanda,
ud3.3:8.2 | S: “A light, it appears to me, has arisen in this quarter, Ānanda; a brightness has arisen. | T: “This direction seems bright to me, Ānanda. This direction seems dazzling to me.
ud3.3:8.3 | S: I’m not put off at the thought of going to where the mendicants are staying on the bank of the Vaggumudā River. | T: It’s not at all repugnant for me to go & pay attention to where the monks on the bank of the River Vaggamudā are staying.
ud3.3:8.4 | S: Send a message to those mendicants: | T: Send a messenger into their presence to say,
ud3.3:8.5 | S: ‘Venerables, the Teacher summons you. He wants to see you.’” | T: ‘The Teacher calls you, friends. The Teacher wants to see you.’”
ud3.3:9.1 | S: “Yes, sir,” Ānanda replied. He went to one of the mendicants and said, | T: Responding, “As you say, lord,” to the Blessed One, Ven. Ānanda went to a certain monk and said,
ud3.3:9.2 | S: “Please, Reverend, go to the mendicants staying on the bank of the Vaggumudā River and say to them, | T: “Come now, friend. Go to the monks on the bank of the River Vaggamudā and say to them,
ud3.3:9.3 | S: ‘Venerables, the Teacher summons you. He wants to see you.’” | T: ‘The Teacher calls you, friends. The Teacher wants to see you.’”
ud3.3:10.1 | S: “Yes, reverend,” replied that mendicant. | T: Responding, “As you say, friend,” to Ven. Ānanda, the monk–
ud3.3:10.2 | S: Then, as easily as a strong person would extend or contract their arm, he vanished from the Great Wood, in the hall with the peaked roof, and reappeared in front of those mendicants on the bank of the Vaggumudā River. | T: just as a strong man might extend his flexed arm or flex his extended arm–disappeared from the Gabled Pavilion in the Great Forest and appeared in front of the monks on the bank of the River Vaggamudā.
ud3.3:10.3 | S: Then he said to those mendicants, | T: Then he said to them,
ud3.3:10.4 | S: “Venerables, the Teacher summons you. He wants to see you.” | T: “The Teacher calls you, friends. The Teacher wants to see you.”
ud3.3:11.1 | S: “Yes, reverend,” replied those mendicants. They set their lodgings in order and took their bowls and robes. | T: Responding, “As you say, friend,” to the monk, the monks set their lodgings in order and, taking their robes & bowls,
ud3.3:11.2 | S: Then, as easily as a strong person would extend or contract their arm, they vanished from the bank of the Vaggumudā River, and reappeared in the presence of the Buddha in the Great Wood, in the hall with the peaked roof. | T: disappeared from the bank of the River Vaggamudā–just as a strong man might extend his flexed arm or flex his extended arm–and appeared in the presence of the Blessed One in the Gabled Pavilion in the Great Forest.
ud3.3:11.3 | S: But at that time the Buddha was sitting immersed in imperturbable meditation. | T: Now, at that time the Blessed One was sitting in imperturbable concentration [either in the fourth jhāna, the dimension of the infinitude of space, or the dimension of the infinitude of consciousness].
ud3.3:11.4 | S: Then those mendicants thought, | T: The thought occurred to the monks,
ud3.3:11.5 | S: “What kind of meditation is the Buddha practicing right now?” | T: “Now, in which mental dwelling is the Blessed One now residing?”
ud3.3:11.6 | S: They thought, | T: Then they realized,
ud3.3:11.7 | S: “He is practicing the imperturbable meditation.” | T: “He is residing in the imperturbable dwelling.”
ud3.3:11.8 | S: They all sat in imperturbable meditation. | T: So they all sat in imperturbable concentration.
ud3.3:12.1 | S: And then, as the night was getting late, in the first watch of the night, Venerable Ānanda rose from his seat, arranged his robe over one shoulder, raised his cupped palms toward the Buddha and said, | T: Then Ven. Ānanda–when the night was far advanced, at the end of the first watch–got up from his seat, arranged his robe over one shoulder, stood facing the Blessed One, paying homage with his hands placed palm-to-palm over his heart, and said to him,
ud3.3:12.2 | S: “Sir, the night is getting late. It is the first watch of the night, and the visiting mendicants have been sitting long. | T: “The night, lord, is far advanced. The first watch has ended. The visiting monks have been sitting here a long time.
ud3.3:12.3 | S: Sir, please greet the visiting mendicants.” | T: May the Blessed One greet them.”
ud3.3:12.4 | S: But the Buddha kept silent. | T: When this was said, the Blessed One remained silent.
ud3.3:13.1 | S: For a second time, as the night was getting late, in the middle watch of the night, Ānanda rose from his seat, arranged his robe over one shoulder, raised his cupped palms toward the Buddha and said, | T: Then a second time, when the night was far advanced, at the end of the middle watch, Ven. Ānanda got up from his seat, arranged his robe over one shoulder, stood facing the Blessed One, paying homage to him with his hands placed palm-to-palm over his heart, and said to him,
ud3.3:13.2 | S: “Sir, the night is getting late. It is the second watch of the night, and the visiting mendicants have been sitting long. | T: “The night, lord, is far advanced.
ud3.3:13.3 | P: nikkhanto majjhimo yāmo; | T: The middle watch has ended.
ud3.3:13.4 | P: ciranisinnā āgantukā bhikkhū; | T: The visiting monks have been sitting here a long time.
ud3.3:13.5 | S: Sir, please greet the visiting mendicants.” | T: May the Blessed One greet them.”
ud3.3:13.6 | S: But for a second time the Buddha kept silent. | T: When this was said, the Blessed One remained silent.
ud3.3:14.1 | S: For a third time, as the night was getting late, in the last watch of the night, as dawn stirred, bringing joy to the night, Ānanda rose from his seat, arranged his robe over one shoulder, raised his cupped palms toward the Buddha and said, | T: Then a third time, when the night was far advanced, at the end of the last watch, as dawn was approaching and the face of the night was beaming, Ven. Ānanda got up from his seat, arranged his robe over one shoulder, stood facing the Blessed One, paying homage to him with his hands placed palm-to-palm over his heart, and said to him,
ud3.3:14.2 | S: “Sir, the night is getting late. It is the last watch of the night; dawn stirs, bringing joy to the night, and the visiting mendicants have been sitting long. | T: “The night, lord, is far advanced.
ud3.3:14.3 | P: nikkhanto pacchimo yāmo; | T: The last watch has ended.
ud3.3:14.4 | P: uddhasto aruṇo; | T: Dawn is approaching
ud3.3:14.5 | P: nandimukhī ratti; | T: and the face of the night is beaming.
ud3.3:14.6 | P: ciranisinnā āgantukā bhikkhū; | T: The visiting monks have been sitting here a long time.
ud3.3:14.7 | S: Sir, please greet the visiting mendicants.” | T: May the Blessed One greet them.”
ud3.3:15.1 | S: Then the Buddha emerged from that immersion and addressed Ānanda, | T: Then the Blessed One, emerging from his imperturbable concentration, said to Ven. Ānanda,
ud3.3:15.2 | S: “If you’d known, Ānanda, you wouldn’t have said so much. | T: “Ānanda, if you had known, not even that much would have occurred to you (to say).
ud3.3:15.3 | S: Both I and these five hundred mendicants have been sitting in imperturbable meditation.” | T: I, along with all 500 of these monks, have been sitting in imperturbable concentration.”
ud3.3:16.1 | S: Then, understanding this matter, on that occasion the Buddha expressed this heartfelt sentiment: | T: Then, on realizing the significance of that, the Blessed One on that occasion exclaimed:
ud3.3:17.1 | S: “A mendicant who has beaten <j>the thorn of sensuality— | T: In whom they’re defeated– the thorn of sensuality,
ud3.3:17.2 | S: and abuse, killing, and caging— | T: insult, assault, & imprisonment:
ud3.3:17.3 | S: steady as a mountain, imperturbable, | T: Like a mountain standing unperturbed,
ud3.3:17.4 | S: trembles not at pleasure and pain.” | T: he doesn’t quiver from pleasures & pains : a monk.
ud3.3:17.5 | P: Tatiyaṁ. | T: (none)

## ud3.4
ud3.4:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: I have heard that
ud3.4:1.2 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery. | T: on one occasion the Blessed One was staying near Sāvatthī at Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery.
ud3.4:1.3 | S: Now at that time Venerable Sāriputta was sitting not far from the Buddha, cross-legged, his body set straight, and mindfulness brought to the present. | T: And on that occasion Ven. Sāriputta was sitting not far from the Blessed One, his legs crossed, his body held erect, having set mindfulness to the fore.
ud3.4:1.4 | S: The Buddha saw him meditating there. | T: The Blessed One saw Ven. Sāriputta sitting not far away, his legs crossed, his body held erect, having set mindfulness to the fore.
ud3.4:2.1 | S: Then, understanding this matter, on that occasion the Buddha expressed this heartfelt sentiment: | T: Then, on realizing the significance of that, the Blessed One on that occasion exclaimed:
ud3.4:3.1 | S: “As a rocky mountain | T: As a mountain of rock
ud3.4:3.2 | S: is unwavering and well grounded, | T: is unwavering, well-settled,
ud3.4:3.3 | S: so when delusion ends, | T: so a monk whose delusion is ended
ud3.4:3.4 | S: a monk, like a mountain, doesn’t tremble.” | T: doesn’t quiver– just like a mountain.
ud3.4:3.5 | P: Catutthaṁ. | T: (none)

## ud3.5
ud3.5:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: I have heard that
ud3.5:1.2 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery. | T: on one occasion the Blessed One was staying near Sāvatthī at Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery.
ud3.5:1.3 | S: Now at that time Venerable Mahāmoggallāna was sitting not far from the Buddha, cross-legged, with his body straight and mindfulness of the body well-established in himself. | T: And on that occasion Ven. Mahā Moggallāna was sitting not far from the Blessed One, his legs crossed, his body held erect, having mindfulness immersed in the body well-established within.
ud3.5:1.4 | S: The Buddha saw him meditating there. | T: The Blessed One saw Ven. Mahā Moggallāna sitting not far away, his legs crossed, his body held erect, having mindfulness immersed in the body well-established within.
ud3.5:2.1 | S: Then, understanding this matter, on that occasion the Buddha expressed this heartfelt sentiment: | T: Then, on realizing the significance of that, the Blessed One on that occasion exclaimed:
ud3.5:3.1 | S: “With mindfulness of the body established, | T: With mindfulness immersed in the body well-established,
ud3.5:3.2 | S: restrained in the six fields of contact, | T: restrained with regard to the six contact-media–
ud3.5:3.3 | S: a mendicant always immersed in samādhi | T: continually centered, a monk
ud3.5:3.4 | S: would know extinguishment in themselves.” | T: can know unbinding for himself.
ud3.5:3.5 | P: Pañcamaṁ. | T: (none)

## ud3.6
ud3.6:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: I have heard that
ud3.6:1.2 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Rājagaha in the Bamboo Grove, the squirrels’ feeding ground. | T: on one occasion the Blessed One was staying near Rājagaha at the Bamboo Forest, the Squirrels’ refuge.
ud3.6:1.3 | S: Now at that time Venerable Pilindavaccha addressed the mendicants as “lowlifes”. | T: Now at on that occasion Ven. Pilindavaccha went around addressing the monks as if they were outcastes.
ud3.6:1.4 | S: Then several mendicants went up to the Buddha, bowed, sat down to one side, and said to him, | T: So a large number of monks went to the Blessed One and, on arrival, bowed down to him and sat to one side. As they were sitting there they said to him,
ud3.6:1.5 | S: “Sir, Venerable Pilindavaccha addresses the mendicants as ‘lowlifes’.” | T: “Lord, Ven. Pilindavaccha goes around addressing the monks as if they were outcastes.”
ud3.6:2.1 | S: So the Buddha addressed one of the monks, | T: Then the Blessed One told a certain monk,
ud3.6:2.2 | S: “Please, monk, in my name tell the mendicant Pilindavaccha that | T: “Come, monk. In my name, call Pilindavaccha, saying,
ud3.6:2.3 | S: the Teacher summons him.” | T: ‘The Teacher calls you, friend Vaccha.’”
ud3.6:2.4 | S: “Yes, sir,” that monk replied. He went to Pilindavaccha and said to him, | T: Responding, “As you say, lord,” to the Blessed One, the monk went to Ven. Pilindavaccha and on arrival said to him,
ud3.6:2.5 | S: “Reverend Pilindavaccha, the Teacher summons you.” | T: “The Teacher calls you, friend Vaccha.”
ud3.6:3.1 | S: “Yes, reverend,” Pilindavaccha replied. He went to the Buddha, bowed, and sat down to one side. The Buddha said to him: | T: Responding, “As you say, my friend,” to the monk, Ven. Pilindavaccha went to the Blessed One and, on arrival, having bowed down to him, sat to one side. As he was sitting there, the Blessed One said to him,
ud3.6:3.2 | S: “Is it really true, Vaccha, that you addressed the mendicants as ‘lowlifes’?” | T: “Is it true, Pilindavaccha, that you go around addressing the monks as if they were outcastes?”
ud3.6:3.3 | S: “Yes, sir,” he replied. | T: “Yes, lord.”
ud3.6:4.1 | S: Then, having applied his mind to Pilindavaccha’s past lives, the Buddha said to the mendicants, | T: Then the Blessed One, having directed attention to Ven. Pilindavaccha’s previous lives, said to the monks,
ud3.6:4.2 | S: “Mendicants, don’t complain about the mendicant Vaccha. | T: “Don’t take offense at the monk Vaccha.
ud3.6:4.3 | S: He doesn’t address the mendicants as ‘lowlifes’ out of hate. | T: It’s not out of inner hatred that he goes around addressing the monks as if they were outcastes.
ud3.6:4.4 | S: For five hundred lives without interruption he was reborn in a brahmin family. | T: For 500 consecutive lifetimes the monk Vaccha has been born in brahman families.
ud3.6:4.5 | S: For a long time, he has addressed people as ‘lowlife’. | T: For a long time he has been accustomed to addressing people as outcastes.
ud3.6:4.6 | S: That’s why he addresses the mendicants as ‘lowlifes’.” | T: That’s why he goes around addressing the monks as if they were outcastes.”
ud3.6:5.1 | S: Then, understanding this matter, on that occasion the Buddha expressed this heartfelt sentiment: | T: Then, on realizing the significance of that, the Blessed One on that occasion exclaimed:
ud3.6:6.1 | S: “In whom dwells no deceit or conceit, | T: In whom there’s no deceit or conceit,
ud3.6:6.2 | S: rid of greed, unselfish, <j>with no need for hope, | T: his greed ended, unpossessive, free from longing,
ud3.6:6.3 | S: with anger cast aside, quenched: | T: his anger dispelled, his mind unbound:
ud3.6:6.4 | S: they are a brahmin, an ascetic, a mendicant.” | T: He’s a contemplative. He’s a brahman : a monk.
ud3.6:6.5 | P: Chaṭṭhaṁ. | T: (none)


